LINUX_NETWORKING_DAYS = {
41:{"title":"Linux Filesystem","phase":"Linux Administration","status":"published","summary":"The Linux phase began with the filesystem layout: where configuration, logs, variable data, devices and user data live and why location communicates purpose.","architecture":"/ → /etc configuration · /var changing service data/logs · /home users · /usr programs/data · /tmp temporary · /dev devices · /proc,/sys kernel views","how":["Linux does not organize the filesystem as a collection of drive letters. A single directory tree gives files a location based on role and mount point.","Knowing the conventional purpose of `/etc`, `/var`, `/home`, `/usr`, `/tmp`, `/dev`, `/proc` and `/sys` makes administration safer because you can predict where a service stores configuration, mutable data and runtime information.","Mount points can place different filesystems under the same tree, so a path that looks nearby can live on a completely different device or storage backend."],"commands":"pwd\nls -lah /\nfindmnt\ndf -hT","verify":"Use `findmnt` and `df -hT` to map important paths to their real filesystems, then inspect the service directories you plan to automate before editing anything.","gotcha":"Path proximity does not mean storage proximity. `/var` may be a separate filesystem even though it appears directly under `/`.","security":"Permissions, mount options and ownership all depend on where data lives. Sensitive configuration and writable application data should not be treated as interchangeable.","lesson":"Filesystem literacy turns 'where did Linux put it?' into a question you can usually answer from convention and mounts.","evidence":"Published journey topic; expanded technical record."},
42:{"title":"Linux Permissions","phase":"Linux Administration","status":"published","summary":"Permissions and ownership determine which users and groups can read, modify or execute files and directories.","architecture":"user identity + group membership → owner/group/other mode bits → allowed filesystem operation","how":["Traditional Unix permissions apply read, write and execute bits separately to owner, group and others. On directories, those bits have directory-specific meaning: execute controls traversal, read controls listing and write controls entry creation/removal when other conditions allow it.","Ownership is as important as numeric modes. A configuration file with `600` is not safe if it is owned by the wrong account, and recursively changing ownership can break services that rely on specific identities.","Special modes such as setuid, setgid and the sticky bit exist for particular use cases and deserve deliberate review because they change normal permission behavior."],"commands":"ls -l PATH\nstat PATH\nchmod 640 file\nchown root:app file","verify":"Check both `ls -l` and `stat`, then test access using the actual service or user identity rather than assuming the mode string tells the whole story.","gotcha":"Directory write permission can allow deleting a file even when the file itself is not writable, because deletion changes the directory entry.","security":"Avoid world-writable files and careless recursive chmod/chown. Apply the minimum access required to the exact path.","lesson":"Permissions are an access-control model, not a collection of magic numbers to copy from Stack Overflow.","evidence":"Published journey topic; expanded technical record."},
43:{"title":"Users, Groups & sudo","phase":"Linux Administration","status":"published","summary":"Linux identity management separates users, groups and delegated privilege so administrative access does not have to mean sharing root.","architecture":"login identity → UID/GIDs → filesystem/process permissions → sudo policy → privileged command","how":["Users receive a UID and primary/supplementary groups. Services also use dedicated identities so compromised processes do not automatically inherit broad access.","`sudo` delegates specific privileged operations according to policy and provides an audit trail better than handing everyone the root password.","Group membership should express an actual access requirement. Powerful groups such as `docker` can effectively grant root-equivalent control even when the username never appears in sudoers."],"commands":"id USER\ngetent passwd USER\ngetent group GROUP\nsudo -l\nsudo visudo -c","verify":"Inspect the user's effective groups and `sudo -l`, then test the exact delegated command. Confirm an unrelated privileged action remains denied.","gotcha":"Adding a user to a privileged group can bypass the careful sudo policy you just wrote. Privilege is the union of all access paths.","security":"Use dedicated service accounts, least-privilege sudo rules and secure group membership review. Edit sudoers through `visudo` or validated drop-ins.","lesson":"Identity design is stronger when privilege is specific, attributable and revocable.","evidence":"Published journey topic; expanded technical record."},
44:{"title":"Processes & Signals","phase":"Linux Administration","status":"published","summary":"Processes are running programs with identities, resources and parent-child relationships; signals are one of the operating system's ways to request or force state changes.","architecture":"parent process → child PID → scheduler/resources · signal sender → target PID → process signal handling","how":["A process has a PID, parent, user identity, open files and runtime state. Tools such as `ps`, `pgrep`, `top` and `/proc` expose different views of that state.","Signals are not all equivalent. SIGTERM requests graceful termination and gives an application a chance to clean up; SIGKILL cannot be caught and ends the process immediately.","A service supervisor such as systemd should normally own long-running service lifecycle. Killing a child process manually can fight the supervisor, which may simply start it again."],"commands":"ps -ef\npgrep -a nginx\nkill -TERM PID\nkill -KILL PID","verify":"Send SIGTERM to a disposable test process and observe cleanup/exit, then compare with SIGKILL only where safe. Inspect the exit status and service supervisor behavior.","gotcha":"`kill` without a signal does not mean 'force kill'; it sends SIGTERM by default. The command name has encouraged generations of unnecessary SIGKILLs.","security":"Process ownership controls who can signal whom. Avoid running applications as root when they only need ordinary user privileges.","lesson":"Graceful shutdown is part of reliability because abruptly stopping a process can turn an application problem into data corruption or incomplete work.","evidence":"Published journey topic; expanded technical record."},
45:{"title":"systemd","phase":"Linux Administration","status":"published","summary":"systemd manages service lifecycle, dependencies and startup state so long-running processes are supervised rather than left to terminal sessions.","architecture":"unit file → systemd manager → process lifecycle + dependencies + restart policy","how":["Service units describe how a process starts, stops and relates to other units. `systemctl` talks to the service manager rather than manipulating the process directly.","`enable` controls whether a unit is wanted during boot; `start` controls the current runtime state. They are separate operations, which is why `enable --now` is useful when both are intended.","Restart policies can improve resilience but also hide crash loops. A service that restarts forever is not healthy simply because `systemctl` keeps producing a PID."],"commands":"systemctl status SERVICE\nsystemctl cat SERVICE\nsudo systemctl enable --now SERVICE\njournalctl -u SERVICE -b","verify":"Inspect the effective unit, start/restart it, then confirm both process state and application behavior. Reboot testing is the evidence for boot-time expectations.","gotcha":"`active (running)` means systemd has a running process, not that the application can satisfy a real request or reach its dependencies.","security":"Use unit hardening options where appropriate, run services under dedicated users and keep Environment files or credentials permissioned correctly.","lesson":"A service manager supervises processes; health still belongs to the application path.","evidence":"Published journey topic; expanded technical record."},
46:{"title":"journald","phase":"Linux Administration","status":"published","summary":"journald turns service and kernel events into queryable operational evidence instead of leaving troubleshooting to memory and guesswork.","architecture":"kernel/services → journald → indexed journal → journalctl filters","how":["The system journal collects messages with metadata such as unit, PID, boot and priority. That metadata lets `journalctl` answer focused questions without grepping every log on the server.","Filtering by unit and boot is especially useful after a service restart or reboot because it narrows the timeline to the exact lifecycle being investigated.","Logs should be treated as evidence but not automatically as truth about the entire system. A missing application log may mean the request never reached the application at all."],"commands":"journalctl -u nginx -b\njournalctl -p warning..alert -b\njournalctl --since '30 min ago'","verify":"Trigger a harmless known event, then retrieve it with a targeted filter and confirm timestamp/unit metadata match the action.","gotcha":"If persistent journal storage is not configured, older logs may disappear across reboot. Know the retention model before relying on the journal for incident history.","security":"Logs can contain secrets, tokens or personal data. Control journal access and avoid writing sensitive values in the first place.","lesson":"Troubleshooting improves when observations have timestamps and sources instead of stories.","evidence":"Published journey topic; expanded technical record."},
47:{"title":"Cron vs systemd Timers","phase":"Linux Administration","status":"published","summary":"Scheduled work needs more than a time expression: execution environment, missed-run behavior, logging and dependencies determine whether a scheduler is reliable.","architecture":"schedule → cron daemon OR systemd timer → job/service → logs/result","how":["Cron is simple and widely available for time-based commands. systemd timers integrate scheduling with service units, journal logging, dependencies and options such as persistent catch-up after a machine was offline.","The environment under a scheduler is usually smaller than an interactive shell. Scripts that only work because your personal PATH, working directory or shell profile happens to exist are fragile.","Choose based on operational needs, not fashion. A tiny independent job can be fine in cron; a service-oriented job that needs dependency management and journal visibility may fit a timer better."],"commands":"crontab -l\nsystemctl list-timers --all\nsystemctl cat myjob.timer\njournalctl -u myjob.service","verify":"Schedule a test execution, confirm the exact command environment and inspect its logs. For a timer using persistence, test the intended missed-run behavior in a lab.","gotcha":"A job that succeeds interactively can fail under cron because PATH, current directory and environment variables are different.","security":"Use dedicated service identities and protect scripts from modification by accounts that should not gain the scheduler's privileges.","lesson":"Scheduling is an execution environment, not merely a clock.","evidence":"Published journey topic; expanded technical record."},
48:{"title":"apt & dnf","phase":"Linux Administration","status":"published","summary":"Package managers resolve repositories, dependencies and installed state, turning software lifecycle into something the operating system can track.","architecture":"configured repositories → package metadata/dependencies → apt/dnf → installed database/files","how":["APT is common on Debian-family systems and DNF on modern RPM-family systems. Both manage package metadata, dependency resolution, installation, upgrade and removal through distribution repositories.","Updating package metadata and upgrading installed software are distinct operations. Production patching should also account for service restarts, kernel changes and application compatibility rather than equating 'command returned 0' with 'maintenance finished.'","Package-management output and return codes matter when automation wraps these tools. A package manager may fail because of locks, repository state or dependency problems that require different responses."],"commands":"sudo apt update\napt list --upgradable\nsudo dnf check-update\ndnf history","verify":"Inspect the installed version through the package manager and confirm the relevant service still starts and behaves correctly after an upgrade.","gotcha":"Package metadata refresh and package upgrade are not the same action. Also, automation should account for package-manager return-code semantics instead of treating every non-zero code identically.","security":"Use trusted repositories, verify signing configuration and patch deliberately. Removing a package should not leave a forgotten exposed service or stale credentials behind.","lesson":"Patch management is system change management, not a ritual command copied into a maintenance window.","evidence":"Published journey topic; expanded technical record."},
49:{"title":"SSH Deep Dive","phase":"Linux Administration","status":"published-artifact","summary":"SSH combines remote access, key authentication and host verification, but small permission or trust mistakes can make secure access fail or become unsafe.","architecture":"SSH client → host-key verification + authentication negotiation → sshd → remote session","how":["SSH first needs to establish that the server identity is acceptable to the client, then negotiate an authentication method for the user. Host keys and user keys solve different trust questions.","Key-based authentication avoids repeatedly sending a reusable password to each remote host. An Ed25519 key can be generated with `ssh-keygen` and the public key installed for the intended account.","Verbose client output is one of the fastest ways to understand failed authentication because it shows configuration, candidate keys and methods the client is actually trying."],"commands":"ssh user@server_ip\nssh -p 2222 user@server_ip\nssh -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 user@server_ip\nssh -v user@server_ip\nssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C 'admin@laptop'","verify":"Connect with verbose output in a lab, confirm the expected host key and authentication key are used, then remove or disable an obsolete credential and prove it no longer works.","gotcha":"Private-key and `.ssh` permissions can cause OpenSSH to reject keys. Another dangerous shortcut is bypassing host-key verification instead of understanding why the server identity changed.","security":"Protect private keys with filesystem permissions and passphrases where appropriate, disable unnecessary password/root login, and keep host-key verification enabled.","lesson":"Remote access is two trust decisions: is this the server I intended, and am I an identity this server should authorize?","evidence":"Published Day 49 infographic artifact recovered from the project archive."},
50:{"title":"Bash Scripting Automation","phase":"Linux Administration","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Bash turns repeated Linux commands into automation only when inputs, quoting, exit behavior and failure visibility are designed deliberately.","architecture":"arguments/environment → Bash script → validated commands → exit status/log output","how":["The published backup example starts with `#!/usr/bin/env bash` and `set -euo pipefail`, then validates required source and destination arguments before doing work.","Quoting parameter expansions prevents spaces and wildcard characters from being reinterpreted unexpectedly. Explicit usage errors and meaningful exit codes make scripts understandable to both humans and calling automation.","Strict mode is helpful but not a substitute for reasoning. You still need to understand commands whose non-zero status is expected, pipeline behavior and cleanup paths."],"commands":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nSOURCE_DIR=\"${1:-}\"\nDEST_DIR=\"${2:-}\"\nif [[ -z \"$SOURCE_DIR\" || -z \"$DEST_DIR\" ]]; then\n  echo \"Usage: $0 SOURCE_DIR DEST_DIR\" >&2\n  exit 2\nfi","verify":"Run the script with valid arguments, missing arguments and a deliberately failing operation. Confirm the output and exit code communicate each state correctly.","gotcha":"`set -e` has context-dependent behavior and does not turn Bash into exception-safe code. Handle expected failures explicitly instead of relying on folklore about strict mode.","security":"Quote variables, validate untrusted input, avoid eval-style construction and keep secrets out of command-line arguments where they may appear in process listings.","lesson":"Reliable shell automation makes failure explicit instead of silently continuing.","evidence":"Published Day 50 infographic artifacts recovered from the project archive."},
51:{"title":"Linux Networking Tools","phase":"Linux Administration","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Linux networking tools become useful when used in an order that proves interfaces, routes, listeners and application reachability instead of as random commands.","architecture":"interface → route → local socket → network path → application protocol → packet capture","how":["Start with local state: `ip addr` shows interfaces/addresses and `ip route` shows how the kernel intends to reach a destination. `ss` then answers whether a process is actually listening on the expected address and port.","Connectivity tests should become progressively more specific. Ping can show ICMP reachability where permitted, while `curl` proves an HTTP request/response path rather than merely proving a host exists.","Packet capture with a narrow filter is the final microscope when higher-level tools disagree. It lets you see whether packets leave, return and carry the flags/protocol you expected."],"commands":"ip addr\nip route get 1.1.1.1\nss -lntup\nping -c 3 HOST\ncurl -v https://example.com\nsudo tcpdump -ni any host HOST","verify":"Choose one real service and trace it in order from local interface through route, listening socket and application request. Record the observation at each layer.","gotcha":"A successful ping does not prove the application port is open, and a failed ping does not prove the application is unreachable because ICMP may be filtered.","security":"Packet captures can contain credentials or sensitive payload metadata. Filter tightly, protect capture files and remove them when no longer needed.","lesson":"Prove one layer, then move to the next.","evidence":"Published Day 51 journey artifact/teaser continuity and project record."},
52:{"title":"Linux Hardening","phase":"Linux Administration","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Linux hardening reduces exposed services and privilege while keeping the effective security state observable and supportable.","architecture":"service inventory → remove/disable unnecessary services → firewall default deny → SSH/identity controls → MAC policy → verification","how":["The first step is inventory: `ss -lntup` and running-service listings reveal what the host is actually exposing. Every listening port should have an owner and a reason.","Unused services can be stopped/disabled, with masking reserved for cases where a service must not be started manually or as a dependency. UFW can then enforce default-deny inbound behavior while allowing only required services.","Mandatory-access-control systems such as AppArmor or SELinux should be inspected and understood. Disabling them because a denial appeared trades a useful security boundary for convenience."],"commands":"sudo ss -lntup\nsystemctl --type=service --state=running\nsudo ufw default deny incoming\nsudo ufw default allow outgoing\nsudo ufw allow OpenSSH\nsudo ufw enable\nsudo ufw status verbose\nsudo aa-status\ngetenforce\nsestatus","verify":"From another host, test only the intended ports. On the server, re-run listener and firewall inspection and confirm disabled services do not return after reboot.","gotcha":"A firewall rule cannot protect a service you forgot was listening on another interface or port. Inventory comes before hardening claims.","security":"Use key-based SSH, least privilege, patching, service minimization and effective-state verification rather than one-time checklist completion.","lesson":"A hardened server runs less, exposes less and makes each remaining privilege deliberate.","evidence":"Published Day 52 artifact and project record."},
53:{"title":"TCP vs UDP","phase":"Networking Deep Dive","status":"published-artifact","summary":"TCP provides a reliable ordered byte stream; UDP provides independent best-effort datagrams with lower protocol overhead and different application responsibilities.","architecture":"TCP: SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK → ordered byte stream\nUDP: application datagram → best-effort delivery","how":["TCP is connection-oriented and tracks sequence, acknowledgement, retransmission, flow control and congestion control. Applications receive a byte stream rather than preserved message boundaries.","UDP is connectionless at the transport layer: there is no built-in delivery, ordering or retransmission guarantee, and datagram boundaries are preserved.","Protocol choice is not simply 'reliable versus fast.' Applications such as QUIC build sophisticated reliability and congestion behavior above UDP because UDP gives them different control over transport semantics."],"commands":"ss -lntup\nnc -vz HOST 443\nsudo tcpdump -ni any 'tcp port 443 or udp port 53'","verify":"Capture a TCP connection and identify SYN, SYN-ACK and ACK; compare with a UDP DNS exchange and observe the lack of a TCP-style connection handshake.","gotcha":"TCP is a byte stream. One sender `send()` does not guarantee one receiver `recv()` with the same message boundary.","security":"Both protocols need explicit firewall policy and application validation. UDP spoofing/amplification risks and long-lived TCP resource usage create different attack surfaces.","lesson":"Transport semantics determine what reliability work the application can assume and what it must provide itself.","evidence":"Published Day 53 infographic artifact."},
54:{"title":"DNS & Cloud Security","phase":"Networking Deep Dive","status":"published-artifact","summary":"DNS turns names into records through caches and recursive resolution, making name resolution both an availability dependency and a security boundary.","architecture":"application → OS resolver/cache → recursive resolver → root → TLD → authoritative nameserver → record","how":["When a name is not already cached, a recursive resolver follows referrals through the DNS hierarchy until it obtains an answer from an authoritative source or proves no answer exists.","Common record types include A, AAAA, CNAME, MX, TXT and NS. TTL values influence how long resolvers may cache answers, which is why DNS changes are not necessarily observed everywhere at once.","Cloud applications often depend on DNS for load balancers, service endpoints, certificates and failover. Treating DNS as 'just a name' hides how many systems trust its answers."],"commands":"dig example.com A\ndig +trace example.com\nhost example.com\nnslookup example.com","verify":"Compare authoritative and recursive answers, inspect TTLs and test from more than one resolver when diagnosing propagation or stale-cache behavior.","gotcha":"Changing a DNS record does not invalidate every cache instantly. An old answer may remain valid until its TTL expires.","security":"Use registrar/account MFA, restrict DNS changes, monitor critical records and deploy DNSSEC where the threat model and provider support justify it.","lesson":"If users reach a service by name, DNS is part of the service architecture.","evidence":"Published Day 54 infographic artifact."},
55:{"title":"DHCP","phase":"Networking Deep Dive","status":"published-artifact","summary":"DHCP automatically leases clients network configuration through the Discover, Offer, Request and Acknowledge exchange.","architecture":"client UDP 68 ⇄ server UDP 67 · Discover → Offer → Request → ACK","how":["A new DHCPv4 client begins without enough configuration for ordinary unicast networking, so discovery commonly relies on broadcast behavior to locate available servers.","The DORA sequence negotiates an offered address and associated options such as subnet mask/prefix, default gateway, DNS servers and lease duration.","A lease is temporary configuration with a lifecycle. Clients renew rather than treating an assigned address as permanent identity."],"commands":"ip addr\nip route\nresolvectl status\nsudo tcpdump -ni any 'port 67 or port 68'","verify":"Capture a DHCP lease acquisition in a lab and identify client/server direction for Discover, Offer, Request and ACK.","gotcha":"The port direction is easy to reverse: DHCP servers use UDP 67 and clients use UDP 68.","security":"Untrusted DHCP servers can provide malicious gateway or DNS configuration. Network access controls such as DHCP snooping may be appropriate on managed switching infrastructure.","lesson":"Automatic addressing is a protocol conversation, not a magical property of connecting a cable.","evidence":"Published Day 55 infographic artifact."},
56:{"title":"HTTP vs HTTPS","phase":"Networking Deep Dive","status":"published-artifact","summary":"HTTPS is HTTP carried inside a TLS-protected connection, adding confidentiality, integrity and authenticated server identity to the transport path.","architecture":"HTTP: client ⇄ TCP ⇄ server\nHTTPS: client ⇄ TLS over transport ⇄ HTTP messages ⇄ server","how":["HTTP defines methods, headers, status codes and message bodies. Plain HTTP does not itself protect those messages from network observers or modification.","HTTPS establishes TLS first and then carries the HTTP exchange inside the protected channel. Ports 80 and 443 are common conventions, not the definition of the protocols themselves.","TLS protects the channel. It does not prove the website is ethical, bug-free or safe from application vulnerabilities."],"commands":"curl -v http://example.com\ncurl -v https://example.com\nopenssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -servername example.com","verify":"Inspect an HTTP redirect to HTTPS, then validate the certificate/handshake and application response independently.","gotcha":"A valid certificate proves a hostname/trust-chain relationship for the channel, not that the application content deserves trust.","security":"Redirect plaintext HTTP where appropriate, mark sensitive cookies Secure, avoid mixed content and deploy HSTS only after HTTPS works for the intended scope.","lesson":"Encryption changes the trust boundary of the transport path, not the quality of the application behind it.","evidence":"Published Day 56 infographic artifact."},
57:{"title":"TLS 1.3 Handshake","phase":"Networking Deep Dive","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Before encrypted application traffic starts, TLS negotiates keys and lets the client validate the server certificate chain and requested hostname.","architecture":"ClientHello(SNI,key share) → ServerHello → certificate + proof → Finished ⇄ encrypted application traffic","how":["A simplified TLS 1.3 handshake begins with ClientHello parameters such as supported protocol versions and key share, usually including the requested hostname through SNI.","The server selects parameters, sends its certificate chain and proves possession of the corresponding private key. The client validates hostname, validity period, signatures and a chain terminating at a trusted certificate.","After handshake completion, HTTP or another application protocol travels using negotiated traffic keys. If TLS terminates at a reverse proxy, the proxy-to-origin leg becomes a separate security decision."],"commands":"openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -servername example.com -showcerts\ncurl -Iv https://example.com","verify":"Check the requested hostname, certificate subject/SANs, issuer chain and verification result. Test SNI explicitly when multiple hostnames share an endpoint.","gotcha":"A server can present a valid leaf certificate and still fail clients if it does not provide the intermediate chain needed to build trust.","security":"Protect private keys, renew certificates before expiry, secure proxy-to-origin traffic when it crosses an untrusted network and do not weaken verification to silence errors.","lesson":"The browser padlock describes channel protection, not a background check on the site operator.","evidence":"Published Day 57 infographic artifact."},
58:{"title":"Nginx Reverse Proxy","phase":"Networking Deep Dive","status":"published-artifact","summary":"A reverse proxy accepts client traffic and forwards it to application backends, moving routing, TLS and client-identity trust into an explicit infrastructure boundary.","architecture":"client → TLS/Nginx reverse proxy → loopback/private backend","how":["Nginx can terminate TLS, route by hostname/path and forward requests to application processes that are not directly exposed to the internet.","Forwarded headers such as Host, X-Forwarded-Proto and X-Forwarded-For preserve context the backend may need, but the application must know which proxy sources are trusted before believing client-supplied forwarding headers.","Configuration should be validated before reload, then the full path inspected with both HTTP tools and logs rather than stopping at 'nginx -t succeeded.'"],"commands":"sudo nginx -t\nsudo systemctl reload nginx\ncurl -I https://example.com\nsudo tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log\nsudo ss -lntp","verify":"Send a request through the public hostname, correlate it in proxy and application logs, then confirm the backend is not unintentionally exposed on a public interface.","gotcha":"Blindly trusting X-Forwarded-For lets a direct client claim an arbitrary source address. Trust only forwarding headers from known proxy networks/configuration.","security":"Bind internal services to loopback/private interfaces where appropriate, protect proxy-to-backend traffic across untrusted networks and maintain explicit trusted-proxy configuration.","lesson":"A reverse proxy can strengthen architecture only when routing and trust boundaries are deliberate.","evidence":"Published Day 58 infographic artifact."},
59:{"title":"Load Balancing Concepts","phase":"Networking Deep Dive","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Load balancing spreads requests across backends, but availability depends on health semantics, session state and every shared dependency that remains.","architecture":"client → load balancer → app1 / app2 → shared dependencies","how":["Round robin distributes requests across available servers, while least-connections selects based on active connections and can better fit workloads with very different request durations.","A process accepting TCP connections is not proof that its database or downstream dependencies are healthy. Passive failure handling and application-level health checks answer different questions.","State stored only in one application process can make users appear logged out when a later request lands on another backend. Stateless applications or shared state stores are usually easier to scale and recover."],"commands":"# Nginx upstream example\nupstream app_backend {\n  server 10.0.1.10:8000;\n  server 10.0.1.11:8000;\n}\n# Inspect:\ncurl -I https://example.com\nss -lntp","verify":"Take one backend out of service and observe request behavior. Then test failure of shared dependencies such as database, Redis or the load balancer itself.","gotcha":"Two application servers do not automatically create high availability if one Nginx instance, database, Redis service, network path or availability zone can still stop the service.","security":"Keep backend exposure private, authenticate sensitive health/admin endpoints and avoid putting session or secret data in client routing tricks without understanding the tradeoffs.","lesson":"Load balancing is traffic control, not a spell that makes every dependency immortal.","evidence":"Published Day 59 infographic artifact."},
60:{"title":"Network Troubleshooting Sequence","phase":"Networking Deep Dive","status":"published-artifact","summary":"Troubleshooting becomes repeatable when a failed request is traced through DNS, routing, transport, TLS, proxy, backend and system pressure one layer at a time.","architecture":"DNS → route → port/transport → TLS → HTTP/proxy → backend/dependencies → disk","how":["Start with the name. If DNS is wrong, testing the application at the expected hostname is already testing the wrong destination. Next prove the route and whether the target port is reachable/listening.","Only after transport works should TLS and HTTP behavior become the focus. Proxy logs, application logs and backend dependency checks then narrow failures inside the service path.","System pressure belongs in the sequence too. Full filesystems or inodes can break logging, temporary files, databases and deployments even when the network path itself is healthy."],"commands":"dig +short core-shield.enthernetservice.com\nip route get DEST_IP\ntraceroute DEST_IP\nnc -vz DEST_IP 443\nopenssl s_client -connect HOST:443 -servername HOST\ncurl -v https://HOST\ndf -h\ndf -i\nsudo du -xhd1 /var | sort -h\nsudo lsof +L1","verify":"For one incident or lab failure, write down the observation at every layer and stop as soon as the first layer fails. Repair it, then continue from that layer forward.","gotcha":"`df` can show space consumed that `du` cannot find when a process still holds a deleted file open. `lsof +L1` is the clue.","security":"Troubleshooting commands and logs can expose internal addresses, headers and secrets. Collect the minimum evidence needed and protect incident artifacts.","lesson":"Prove one layer, record the evidence, then move to the next.","evidence":"Published Day 60 infographic artifact; closes the networking phase."}
}
